    bottomLine:
      "The pick for walkers who need the machine out of the way between sessions but refuse to give up a 60-inch deck.",
    description:
      "Folding usually costs you deck length, and for walkers that is the wrong thing to give up. The 995i is the cleanest exception we tested: a full 20 x 60-inch surface on a frame that still folds upright into a footprint you can push against a wall. The hydraulic assist is the part that decides whether a folding treadmill actually gets folded, and here it is good enough that our testers used it daily rather than giving up after a week. The cushioning is the honest weak point — it is soft out of the box and measurably firmer after four weeks, which matters more to a walker logging an hour a day than to a runner doing 20 minutes. If folding is your central requirement, our folding treadmill guide compares the mechanisms directly.",
  },
  {
    id: "sole-f65",
    name: "Sole F65",
    category: "Best for Two-Walker Households",
    badge: "Best for Couples",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Sole+F65+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: "/sole-f65-treadmill/",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 3760,
    priceRange: "$1,199-$1,399",
    specs: "3.25 CHP · 20 x 60 in. deck · 0-15% incline · 325 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "Heavier frame absorbs two people's daily mileage without complaint",
      "Cushioning held up best of anything we tested over eight weeks",
      "Still no subscription requirement anywhere in the console",
      "Genuinely quiet at walking speed once the belt is bedded in",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Roughly $300 more than the F63 for changes most solo walkers won't feel",
