      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="format" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Full Treadmill or Walking Pad?</h2>
          <p>
            Every machine above is a full-size treadmill, and for a meaningful
            group of readers that is the wrong category entirely. Walking pads
            — the flat, deckless slabs that slide under a desk or a sofa — have
            taken over a large share of the walking market, and they are
            genuinely good at one job: accumulating steps at low speed while
            your attention is elsewhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            The honest split is this. If your goal is to be walking while you
            work, at 1.5 to 3 mph, and the machine needs to disappear when you
            are done, a walking pad is the better purchase and a full treadmill
            will mostly sit unused. If your goal is a daily walking workout —
            something with an intensity you can increase over time — the pad's
            missing incline, short deck and thin cushioning become hard limits
            within a few months. We have taken this apart properly in
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs. treadmill</a>,
            and reviewed the pads themselves in our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            A pattern worth naming: a fair number of readers end up owning
            both, and it is not as absurd as it sounds. The pad handles the
            weekday step count during working hours; the full treadmill handles
            the deliberate incline session. If you are choosing one first, buy
            for the habit you already have rather than the one you hope to
            acquire.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Incline: A Walker's Substitute for Speed</h2>
          <p>
            Incline is the most under-used feature on home treadmills and the
            most valuable one for anybody who walks. Raising the deck to 10%
            roughly doubles the metabolic cost of walking at a given speed
            without adding any of the joint impact that running brings. For
            walkers who have plateaued — the ones who tell us their forty-five
            minutes stopped feeling like anything after a few months — incline
            is almost always the answer, and it is available on a machine they
            already own.
          </p>
          <p>
            The best-known structured version is the 12-3-30 session: 12%
            incline, 3 mph, 30 minutes. Its popularity is deserved because it
            is specific, repeatable and hard enough to produce a training
            effect, but it is a genuinely demanding session and the number of
            people who attempt it cold and abandon it in week one is high. Our
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 guide</a>
            sets out a four-week build-up and the modifications that make it
            work for people who are not starting from a base of fitness.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two practical constraints. First, most treadmills top out at 10 to
            15%, so check the number before you commit to a protocol that
            requires 12. Second, holding the handrails through an incline walk
            reduces the workload considerably — that is fine as a stability
            measure and self-defeating if you are leaning on them. If steep
            walking is going to be your main mode, a dedicated incline trainer
            reaching 40% is a different proposition altogether; our
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline treadmill roundup</a>
            covers that tier in full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Placement, Noise and Living With It</h2>
          <p>
            The most common reason a treadmill stops being used is not the
            machine. It is where the machine ended up. A treadmill in a spare
            room two floors away gets used for a month; a treadmill in the room
            where you already spend time gets used for years. That argues for
            buying the machine that fits where you will actually walk, even if
            a better machine would fit somewhere you will not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Noise is the constraint that decides placement in most homes, and
            it is widely misunderstood. The sound that annoys the people around
            you is rarely the motor — at walking speed a modern treadmill motor
            is close to inaudible over a television. What travels is impact:
            footfall energy passing through the deck, into the frame, into the
            floor and out through the structure of the building. That is why a
            heavier machine with better cushioning can be less disruptive than
            a lighter, quieter-on-paper one, and why a proper rubber mat
            underneath makes a bigger difference than any specification. Our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to quiet treadmills for apartments</a>
            goes through what actually works.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also measure your ceiling before buying anything with a steep
            incline. Standing on a deck raised to 15% puts your head several
            inches higher than standing on the floor, and at 40% on an incline
            trainer the difference is substantial. Basements with low joists
            and rooms with pendant lighting are where this becomes a genuine
            problem rather than a theoretical one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, think about folding honestly. A folding treadmill that is
            awkward to fold gets folded twice and then never again. If the
            machine has to move between sessions, prioritise the fold mechanism
            over almost everything else — our
            <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding mechanism primer</a>
            explains which designs survive daily use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Each Budget Band Buys</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Under $800.</strong> You are choosing between a 55-inch
            deck on a branded frame and a 60-inch deck on an unbranded one. For
            walkers under about 5 foot 10, the branded frame is usually the
            better bet because frame quality shows up as noise and belt
            tracking over time. Expect a 10% incline ceiling and a basic
            console. See our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$1,000 guide</a>
            for the full field at this level.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>$800 to $1,300.</strong> This is where walking treadmills
            get genuinely good, and where we would tell most walkers to buy.
            You get a 60-inch deck, 12 to 15% incline, cushioning that survives
            daily use, and a frame heavy enough to stay quiet. Both Sole
            machines and the ProForm sit here. Our
            <a href="/treadmills-under-1500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$1,500 roundup</a>
            covers the adjacent options.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>$1,300 to $2,500.</strong> The money goes to two places:
            screens and incline hardware. Screens do nothing for a walker.
            Incline hardware does a great deal, which is why an incline trainer
            is the only thing in this band we would push a walker toward. If a
            machine in this range is priced by its display, buy the cheaper
            model and keep the difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Above $2,500.</strong> Commercial construction, and the
            single benefit that matters to a walker is consistency: the same
            deck feel, the same belt tracking, year after year, under daily
            hours that would wear out a consumer frame. It is a defensible
            purchase for someone walking an hour a day indefinitely and poor
            value for everyone else. Our
            <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">over-$2,500 guide</a>
            is candid about where that line sits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Final Recommendations</h2>
          <p>
            For most walkers, the <strong>Sole F63</strong> is the machine to
            buy. It gets the three things that matter to a walker right — deck
            length, cushioning that lasts, and a usable incline range — and it
            does not attach a subscription to any of them. Nothing else in the
            test offered a better combination for the money.
          </p>
          <p>
            Below $800, the <strong>NordicTrack C 700</strong> is the
            sensible compromise, provided you are not tall and not planning
            steep incline work. If the machine has to fold away between
            sessions, the <strong>ProForm Power 995i</strong> keeps the full
            deck length that folding usually costs you. Two people walking
            daily should step up to the <strong>Sole F65</strong>. And if
            incline is going to be the point rather than a feature, the
            <strong>NordicTrack X9i</strong> is the only machine here that
            gives walkers somewhere left to progress to.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, spend the first fortnight walking rather than
            optimising. The habit is the hard part; the machine is just the
            thing that makes it convenient.
          </p>
        </div>
