              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need a strong motor if you only walk on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Walking at 2.5 to 4 mph asks very little of a treadmill
                motor, so anything rated 2.5 CHP continuous duty or above has
                ample headroom. Motor size is the wrong spec to optimise for as
                a walker. Deck length, cushioning quality and incline range
                change your daily experience far more, and they are where the
                money is better spent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a walking pad good enough, or do I need a full treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A walking pad is good enough if your goal is accumulating
                low-intensity steps while you work, at speeds below about 3.5
                mph. It is not good enough if you want incline training, a
                comfortable stride at higher walking speeds, or a machine that
                lasts a decade. Most walking pads have short decks, minimal
                cushioning and no incline, which caps how far the habit can
                progress.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should the deck be on a walking treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for 55 inches minimum, and 60 inches if you are over about
                5 foot 10 or plan to walk on incline. Stride length grows as
                incline increases, so a deck that feels fine on the flat can
                feel short at 10% grade. Deck width matters less for walkers
                than for runners; 20 inches is comfortable for nearly everyone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much incline do I need for walking workouts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ten to twelve per cent covers the great majority of
                incline-walking sessions, including the popular 12-3-30
                protocol. Going beyond that only makes sense if you
                specifically intend to train at steep grades, in which case an
                incline trainer reaching 40% gives walkers a progression path
                that does not require increasing speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are walking treadmills quiet enough for an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Walking is the quietest thing you can do on a treadmill, but
                the noise your neighbours hear is mostly impact transmitted
                through the floor rather than motor sound through the air. A
                heavier frame, a well-cushioned deck and a proper rubber mat
                under the machine do more for that than any advertised decibel
                figure.
              </p>
