        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Walkers Make When Buying</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Nearly every regretful purchase we hear about traces back to one of these four. All of them come from applying a runner's buying logic to a walker's use case.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for motor headroom instead of deck length</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 3.5 CHP motor and a 2.75 CHP motor are indistinguishable at 3 mph. A 60-inch deck and a 55-inch deck are not. Buyers routinely spend an extra $300 on the motor spec and then accept the shorter deck, which is the one thing they will notice every single session. Rank deck length first and treat anything above 2.5 CHP as sufficient.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the incline ceiling until after delivery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Incline is the walker's only real progression lever, and plenty of budget machines stop at 10%. If you have a specific protocol in mind — 12-3-30 being the common one — check the maximum grade before you buy, not after. Discovering your machine cannot reach the number the workout requires is a frustrating and entirely avoidable outcome.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging cushioning in the first week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Deck cushioning firms up as the elastomers bed in, and several machines that feel plush on delivery are noticeably harder a month later. For someone walking an hour a day that is the difference between comfort and heel pain. Read long-term owner reports rather than launch reviews, and treat a suspiciously soft out-of-the-box feel as a question rather than a selling point.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the room you wish you walked in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A better treadmill in the basement loses to a decent treadmill in the living room every time, because the one you walk past is the one you use. Decide where the machine will live before you decide which machine it is, then measure that space including ceiling height at full incline. Footprint and placement drive long-term use far more than any spec on the box.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

